## Proof

**Setup and symmetry.** The inequality is invariant under the simultaneous swap $(a, \alpha) \leftrightarrow (b, 1-\alpha)$ (both sides are unchanged), so we may assume $a \ge b > 0$. Set $s = \ln(a/b) \ge 0$.

**Two functions with the same boundary values.** Fix $x \ne 0$ and define on $[0,1]$:
$$F(t) = \frac{a\sinh(tx) + b\sinh((1-t)x)}{\sinh x}, \qquad G(t) = a^t b^{1-t}.$$

Both satisfy $F(0)=G(0)=b$ and $F(1)=G(1)=a$. The desired inequality (with $\alpha = t$) is precisely $G(t) \le F(t)$ for all $t \in (0,1)$.

**Key ODE structure.** A direct computation gives:
$$F''(t) = \frac{a x^2 \sinh(tx) + b x^2 \sinh((1-t)x)}{\sinh x} = x^2\, F(t).$$

For $G$: since $G(t) = b\,(a/b)^t = b\,e^{st}$, we get $G''(t) = s^2\, G(t)$.

**The difference satisfies a forced ODE.** Let $H = F - G$, so $H(0) = H(1) = 0$. Then
$$H''(t) - x^2 H(t) = x^2 F(t) - s^2 G(t) - x^2(F(t)-G(t)) = (x^2 - s^2)\,G(t). \tag{$\ast$}$$

Since $G(t) = b\,e^{st} > 0$, the sign of the right-hand side is the sign of $x^2 - s^2$.

**Maximum principle (sign lemma).** *If $u'' - \mu\, u \le 0$ on $[0,1]$ with $\mu \ge 0$ and $u(0)=u(1)=0$, then $u \ge 0$ on $[0,1]$.*

*Proof.* If $u$ attained a negative value, it would have a negative minimum at some $t_0 \in (0,1)$, where $u(t_0)<0$ and $u''(t_0)\ge 0$. Then $u''(t_0) - \mu\,u(t_0) \ge -\mu\,u(t_0) > 0$, contradicting $u''-\mu u \le 0$. $\square$

The reverse statement (if $u''-\mu u \ge 0$ with zero boundary values, then $u \le 0$) follows by applying the lemma to $-u$.

**Case 1: $|x| \le s$ (i.e., $x^2 \le s^2$).** Then $(x^2 - s^2)G(t) \le 0$, so $(\ast)$ gives $H'' - x^2 H \le 0$ with $H(0)=H(1)=0$ and $x^2 \ge 0$. By the lemma, $H \ge 0$, i.e.,
$$a^t b^{1-t} = G(t) \le F(t) = \frac{a\sinh(tx)+b\sinh((1-t)x)}{\sinh x}, \qquad \forall\, t\in[0,1].$$
This is exactly the required inequality for every $\alpha \in (0,1)$.

**Case 2: $|x| > s$ (i.e., $x^2 > s^2$).** Then $(x^2-s^2)G(t) > 0$, so $H'' - x^2 H \ge 0$ with zero boundary values. By the reverse lemma, $H \le 0$ on $[0,1]$. Moreover $H < 0$ on $(0,1)$: if $H(t_0)=0$ for some interior $t_0$, then $t_0$ is a (nonnegative) maximum with $H(t_0)=0$, so $H''(t_0)\le 0$, giving $H''(t_0)-x^2 H(t_0)=H''(t_0)\le 0$, contradicting the strictly positive right-hand side $(x^2-s^2)G(t_0)>0$. Thus $F(t)<G(t)$ for every $t\in(0,1)$: the inequality **fails** for every $\alpha\in(0,1)$.

**Boundary $|x| = s$.** Here $x^2 = s^2$, so $(\ast)$ becomes $H'' - s^2 H = 0$ with $H(0)=H(1)=0$. The unique solution is $H\equiv 0$, i.e., equality holds for all $\alpha$ — consistent with both cases.

**Conclusion.** The inequality holds for all $\alpha \in (0,1)$ if and only if $0 < |x| \le |\ln(a/b)|$. (When $a=b$, $s=0$ and the admissible set is empty, which is vacuously fine.) The largest such $c$ is therefore

$$\boxed{c = \left|\ln\frac{a}{b}\right| = \left|\ln a - \ln b\right|.}$$

